# --- s034 Book I d.8 q.2 = Ord. I d.8 p.1 q.2 (Vat. IV)
n("n582","s034-01","Secunda questio octaue distinctionis","la","source","= Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 8 p. 1 q. 2, *utrum aliqua creatura sit simplex*.","certain")
n("n583","s034-01","Aug. de tri. c. 5.","la","source","Augustine, *De Trin.* VI 6.8: *creatura… nulla est simplex*. A gives no book number.","to-verify")
n("n584","s034-02","prima parte summe. q. 3. arti. 4.","la","source","Aquinas, *ST* I q. 3 a. 4 (in God alone are essence and *esse* the same) and q. 50 a. 2 ad 3 and ad 4 (angels are composed of *esse* and *quod est*, act and potency). Gorris's references are exact.","certain")
n("n585","s034-02","libro. 2°. contra gentiles. c. 49.","la","source","The composition of act and potency in intellectual creatures is *Summa contra Gentiles* II c. 52–53. Chapter 49 proves that intellectual substance is not a body. The chapter number is therefore doubtful.","to-verify")
n("n586","s034-03","5°. metha. c. de priuatione","la","source","Aristotle, *Metaph.* V.22, 1022b22–33 (*steresis*). The mole (*talpa*) as an example of privation relative to the genus is the medieval commonplace (cf. *Cat.* 10 and *De anima* III.1, 425a10).","certain")
n("n587","s034-02","Doctor improbat","la","source","Scotus rejects the thesis that every creature is composed of act and potency (essence and *esse*). His answer is that every creature is *composita vel componibilis*.","to-verify")
n("n588","s034-02","the holy Doctor [Thomas]","en","translation","*sanctus doctor*: Thomas Aquinas, canonized in 1323.")

